      title: "A Book Spoken to God",
      body: `## The opening

The *Confessions* begin not with "I was born" but with a prayer:

> You are great, O Lord, and greatly to be praised: great is your power, and of your wisdom there is no measure. And a human being wants to praise you, a small part of your creation, a human being who carries his mortality about with him, who carries the evidence of his sin. … You stir us up so that it delights us to praise you, for you have made us for yourself, and our heart is restless until it rests in you.
>
> — *Confessions* 1.1.1

The first sentence is a quotation from the Psalms (Psalm 145:3 and 147:5). The last is perhaps the most famous sentence Augustine ever wrote: *fecisti nos ad te, et inquietum est cor nostrum donec requiescat in te*. In a single line it states the book's theme. Human beings are made toward God (*ad te*), and nothing else will satisfy them. The rest of the book is the story of one restless heart, told to the God in whom it finally found rest.

## Who is being addressed?

The *Confessions* are addressed, from first page to last, to God. Augustine speaks to God in the second person throughout—"you"—and we, the readers, overhear. This is not a pious decoration. It changes everything about the book. Augustine is not trying to tell God anything God does not know: "What could be hidden in me from you, even if I would not confess it?" (10.2.2). He is praising God, and examining himself in God's presence, and he hopes that readers who overhear will be moved to do the same.

## What does "confession" mean?

The Latin word *confessio* had three meanings in Augustine's Christian world, and the book uses all of them.

- **Confession of sin**: acknowledging one's faults before God. Augustine does this with unsparing honesty, about a childhood theft, his sexual life, his ambition, and his pride.
- **Confession of praise**: in the Latin Psalms, *confiteor* often means "I praise" or "I give thanks." When the psalmist says *confitebor tibi, Domine*, he means "I will praise you, Lord." Augustine's book is, above all, a hymn of praise for God's mercy.
- **Confession of faith**: declaring what one believes, as the martyrs, the "confessors," did before their persecutors.

Augustine himself, looking back near the end of his life, said that the thirteen books of the *Confessions* "praise the just and good God for my evil and good deeds, and stir up the human mind and heart toward him" (*Retractations* 2.6). Praise comes first.

## Why read it?

The *Confessions* have been called the first autobiography in Western literature. That is not quite true: there were earlier accounts of lives written in the first person, from Roman generals' memoirs to the self-examination of the Stoic emperor Marcus Aurelius. But nothing earlier resembles this book's searching examination of an inner life: of motives, desires, memories, and the self's divisions against itself. "I had become a great question to myself" (4.4.9), Augustine writes. Sixteen centuries of readers have recognized themselves in that sentence.

The book has been read in many ways: as a devotional classic, by monks and nuns for whom it was a model of prayer; as a work of theology, laying down Augustine's teaching on sin, grace, and the will; as a work of philosophy, especially for its reflections on memory and time in Books 10 and 11; as a historical source for the late Roman world; and as literature. Petrarch carried a pocket copy up Mont Ventoux in 1336, and opened it at the summit. Rousseau, Tolstoy, and countless modern memoirists wrote their own "confessions" in its shadow. The philosopher **Ludwig Wittgenstein** began his *Philosophical Investigations* (1953) by quoting it, and once called it perhaps the most serious book ever written.

## How this course proceeds

This course reads the *Confessions* in order, across five lessons. This first lesson sets the scene—Augustine's Africa, his life, and why he wrote the book—and reads Book 1. Lesson II reads Books 2–4: adolescence, Carthage, the Manichees, and the death of a friend. Lesson III reads Books 5–7: the journey to Rome and Milan, the encounter with Ambrose, and the "books of the Platonists." Lesson IV reads Books 8–9: the conversion in the garden at Milan, the baptism, and the death of Monica at Ostia. Lesson V reads Books 10–13: memory, temptation, time, and creation, and asks what holds the book together and how it has been read since.

Quotations from the *Confessions* are the course's own renderings from Augustine's Latin, made to be clear rather than elegant. They are cited by book, chapter, and paragraph: 1.1.1 means Book 1, chapter 1, paragraph 1. Students should read the whole book alongside the course in a complete translation, ideally **Henry Chadwick**'s (Oxford World's Classics, 1991).

> **Pause and consider.** What changes when a life story is addressed to God rather than to a reader? What can the author say, or not say, as a result?`,
