      title: "Roman Africa",
      body: `## A rich province

Augustine was an **African**. He was born in 354 in **Thagaste** (modern Souk Ahras in Algeria), a small town in the Roman province of Numidia, about sixty miles inland from the Mediterranean. He spent most of his life in Africa, and died there, as bishop of Hippo Regius, in 430. To understand the *Confessions*, we need to picture this world.

Roman North Africa—the coastal lands of modern Tunisia, Algeria, and eastern Morocco—was one of the richest regions of the empire. Its great estates produced grain that fed the city of Rome, and olive oil that was shipped across the Mediterranean. Its cities had forums, temples, baths, theaters, and amphitheaters; the ruins of many, such as Timgad and Djemila, still stand. **Carthage**, refounded by the Romans on the site of the city they had destroyed in 146 BC, was the second city of the Latin-speaking West, after Rome itself, and a great center of law, rhetoric, and entertainment.

## Many peoples, one language of culture

The population was mixed. Most people in the countryside were descended from the indigenous peoples whom the Romans called Numidians and Moors, ancestors of the modern **Berbers** (Amazigh). In some areas, people still spoke a late form of **Punic**, the language of Carthage; Augustine mentions it, and as bishop he needed priests who could speak it to country people. But the language of education, law, and government was **Latin**, and the educated classes of Africa were among the most enthusiastic Latin writers of the empire. Augustine's own family probably had Berber ancestry—his mother's name, Monica, may derive from a local Libyan deity—but they were thoroughly Roman in culture and aspirations.

Greek, by contrast, was a foreign language in Africa. Augustine learned it at school, hated it, and never mastered it fully. This matters for the *Confessions*: Augustine read Greek philosophy largely in Latin translation, and the Bible in Latin.

## A Christian Africa

Africa was also one of the oldest and most fervent centers of Latin Christianity. The first Christian writings in Latin came from Africa: the *Acts of the Scillitan Martyrs* (180), and the works of **Tertullian** of Carthage (c. 160–c. 225), who coined much of the Latin vocabulary of theology. The bishop **Cyprian** of Carthage was martyred in 258. African Christians were proud of their martyrs, and their churches were full of shrines to them.

By Augustine's birth, the emperor Constantine had become a Christian (312), and Christianity was favored by the emperors. But African Christianity was divided. After the great persecution under Diocletian (303–305), a dispute arose over clergy who had handed over the Scriptures to the authorities. The party that refused to accept them and their successors became a separate church, named after its leader **Donatus**. In Augustine's time, the **Donatist** church was probably the majority church in Numidia, with its own bishops in nearly every town; the Catholic church, in communion with Rome and the churches overseas, was its rival. As a bishop, Augustine would spend decades in controversy with the Donatists. In the *Confessions*, interestingly, they hardly appear.

## Old gods and new

Traditional religion was still alive. In Augustine's youth, temples were open, sacrifices were offered, and public festivals honored the old gods, though imperial legislation against sacrifice grew stricter through the later fourth century. The emperor **Julian** (361–363) briefly tried to restore paganism when Augustine was a boy. Many educated people, while not hostile to Christianity, still loved the classical literature that was saturated with the gods. Augustine's education was entirely in that literature.

## Rhetoric and careers

For an ambitious young man without great family wealth, the path to success in the late Roman world lay through **rhetoric**: the art of persuasive speech, mastered through years of studying Latin literature, above all **Virgil** and **Cicero**. A skilled rhetorician could become a teacher, an advocate in the law courts, or an official in the imperial government; with luck and patronage, he might rise to a provincial governorship and senatorial rank. Augustine's education aimed at exactly this, and his early career followed it: he taught rhetoric in Thagaste, Carthage, Rome, and finally Milan, where he became the official public orator of the imperial city.

The *Confessions* are, among other things, the story of how that career was given up. Augustine writes with the full skill of a trained rhetorician—the book is full of balanced antitheses, wordplay, and rhythmic prose—and at the same time with a deep suspicion of rhetoric's power to deceive and its service of ambition. He calls himself, looking back, "a seller of words" (*venditor verborum*, 9.5.13).

## The empire in crisis

Augustine lived through great changes. When he was born, the empire was still united and powerful. In 378, when he was twenty-four, the Goths destroyed a Roman army at **Adrianople**, killing the emperor Valens. In 410, the Goths under Alaric sacked **Rome** itself, a shock that prompted Augustine's great work *The City of God*. When he died in 430, the **Vandals** were besieging Hippo; they soon took Carthage and created a kingdom in Africa. The *Confessions*, written around 397–401, belong to the last years of relative peace in Roman Africa.

> **Pause and consider.** Augustine was an African who wrote in Latin and became one of the most influential figures in European thought. How might his African origins have shaped him, and why might they have been overlooked by later readers?`,
